 "1c73b48a-8fa2-50d0-8f1f-38363c483baf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "A∨(¬B∧¬C)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1bbccb36-dc0d-5510-a61b-7447bfd3d2d7" :answer/id #uuid "1bbccb36-dc0d-5510-a61b-7447bfd3d2d7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "¬A∨(B∧¬C)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3610415a-5c10-5b10-962a-634e7664a552" :answer/id #uuid "3610415a-5c10-5b10-962a-634e7664a552" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "¬A∨(C∧¬B)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3183a560-83c2-5a40-b064-73f7caf64d8c" :question/id #uuid "3183a560-83c2-5a40-b064-73f7caf64d8c" :question/math-academy-id "q-129255" :question/problem "Which statement is equivalent to $(¬P∨R)∧(¬P∨¬Q)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we can factor out the disjunction with $¬P,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n(¬P∨R)∧(¬P∨¬Q)≡¬P∨(R∧¬Q)\n$$\n\nNow, since $R∧¬Q≡¬Q∧R,$ we can write\n\n$$\n¬P∨(R∧¬Q)≡¬P∨(¬Q∧R).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-93fc56d6-03f9-525e-86ef-83d8d950398c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-93fc56d6-03f9-525e-86ef-83d8d950398c" :answer-field/id #uuid "93fc56d6-03f9-525e-86ef-83d8d950398c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1829e68c-6c8e-52c0-b182-3524055f1bdd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-dc362a4d-8163-5403-8740-5893bb9e990e" "answer-736afa87-729a-5f9d-9faa-2155c70bc7eb" "answer-0dc5ad82-1cee-5b00-baa5-dd0e29276e00" "answer-026a5106-85ec-56e7-84bf-af8759e8ee4f" "answer-1829e68c-6c8e-52c0-b182-3524055f1bdd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dc362a4d-8163-5403-8740-5893bb9e990e" :answer/id #uuid "dc362a4d-8163-5403-8740-5893bb9e990e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(Q∧R)∨P" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-736afa87-729a-5f9d-9faa-2155c70bc7eb" :answer/id #uuid "736afa87-729a-5f9d-9faa-2155c70bc7eb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(¬Q∧R)∨P" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0dc5ad82-1cee-5b00-baa5-dd0e29276e00" :answer/id #uuid "0dc5ad82-1cee-5b00-baa5-dd0e29276e00" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "P∨(¬Q∧¬R)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-026a5106-85ec-56e7-84bf-af8759e8ee4f" :answer/id #uuid "026a5106-85ec-56e7-84bf-af8759e8ee4f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "¬P∨(Q∧¬R)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1829e68c-6c8e-52c0-b182-3524055f1bdd" :answer/id #uuid "1829e68c-6c8e-52c0-b182-3524055f1bdd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "¬P∨(¬Q∧R)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b416b846-e38c-522b-bcdc-fa3137a09cdc" :question/id #uuid "b416b846-e38c-522b-bcdc-fa3137a09cdc" :question/math-academy-id "q-128652" :question/problem "Which statement is equivalent to $(C∨¬B)∧(C∨A)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we can factor out the disjunction with $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n(C∨¬B)∧(C∨A)≡C∨(¬B∧A)\n$$\n\nNow, since $¬B∧A≡A∧¬B,$ we can write\n\n$$\nC∨(¬B∧A)≡C∨(A∧¬B).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f6fa8333-4cb7-50a9-8793-2eba0fa65ac3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f6fa8333-4cb7-50a9-8793-2eba0fa65ac3" :answer-field/id #uuid "f6fa8333-4cb7-50a9-8793-2eba0fa65ac3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-13df6a77-4aea-5da6-8387-5ef9f35de116" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c198dbc3-9c28-5880-9cae-6d3de7417097" "answer-c0089ef5-ccac-5ea2-a176-d9778f388da7" "answer-672c3d80-6f1e-5a91-916f-414df7b9ad2f" "answer-13df6a77-4aea-5da6-8387-5ef9f35de116" "answer-137c1b7a-da3e-50d5-8e72-884fdf7d921e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c198dbc3-9c28-5880-9cae-6d3de7417097" :answer/id #uuid "c198dbc3-9c28-5880-9cae-6d3de7417097" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(B∧¬A)∨C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c0089ef5-ccac-5ea2-a176-d9778f388da7" :answer/id #uuid "c0089ef5-ccac-5ea2-a176-d9778f388da7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(¬B∧A)∨¬C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-672c3d80-6f1e-5a91-916f-414df7b9ad2f" :answer/id #uuid "672c3d80-6f1e-5a91-916f-414df7b9ad2f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "C∧(A∧¬B)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-13df6a77-4aea-5da6-8387-5ef9f35de116" :answer/id #uuid "13df6a77-4aea-5da6-8387-5ef9f35de116" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "C∨(A∧¬B)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-137c1b7a-da3e-50d5-8e72-884fdf7d921e" :answer/id #uuid "137c1b7a-da3e-50d5-8e72-884fdf7d921e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "C∨(¬B∨A)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186158481 :tutorial/content "Just like we can distribute multiplication over addition, we can also distribute conjunctions over disjunctions (and vice versa).\n\n- We can distribute a conjunction over a disjunction, as follows:\n\n$$\nA \\land (B \\lor C)  \\equiv (A \\land B) \\lor (A \\land C)\n$$\n\n- Likewise, we can distribute a disjunction over a conjunction, as follows:\n\n$$\nA \\lor (B \\land C)  \\equiv (A \\lor B) \\land (A \\lor C)\n$$\n\nThe rules above are called the **distributive laws** for propositional logic. To prove that these laws hold, we can construct truth tables.\n\n- The following truth table shows that $A \\land (B \\lor C)  \\equiv (A \\land B) \\lor (A \\land C){:}$\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,C\\,$ | $\\, A \\land (B \\lor C) \\,$ | $\\, (A \\land B) \\lor (A \\land C) \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ |\n\n- The following truth table shows that $A \\lor (B \\land C)  \\equiv (A \\lor B) \\land (A \\lor C){:}$\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,C\\,$ | $\\, A \\lor (B \\land C) \\,$ | $\\, (A \\lor B) \\land (A \\lor C) \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ |" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157479 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
